<BaseLayout title="About Mossy Tank" description="Who's behind Mossy Tank, why this site exists, and what you can actually expect from the guides here.">
  <div class="container prose post">
    <h1>About Mossy Tank</h1>

    <p>
      Mossy Tank exists because the internet is full of aquarium advice that assumes you already
      know what you're doing — and very little of it is honest about how many fish and plants
      died along the way.
    </p>

    <p>
      I got into this hobby the way a lot of people do: a YouTube rabbit hole at 1am. I stumbled
      onto Takashi Amano's work and my jaw dropped. I'd kept fish as a kid — the classic
      gravel-and-plastic-castle setup — but seeing an aquascape that looked like a miniature
      ancient forest underwater completely rewired something in my brain. I had a tank set up
      within three weeks.
    </p>

    <p>
      I did not cycle that tank. I added fish after five days because I was impatient and excited,
      and then watched a whole school of neon tetras die while I frantically Googled what was
      happening. That was my introduction to New Tank Syndrome, and it's the reason every beginner
      guide on this site leads with the nitrogen cycle. I felt terrible about it. You don't have to
      make the same mistake.
    </p>

    <h2>What I'm running now</h2>

    <p>
      My main display is a 90cm rimless — around 120 litres — set up as a low-tech Nature Aquarium
      style scape. There's a Monte Carlo carpet across the foreground (which took nearly a year of
      dialing in lighting duration and EI dosing to fully close in — the day it did, I sent photos
      to people who absolutely did not care as much as I did), Rotala rotundifolia going wild in the
      background, and some Ludwigia sp. 'Super Red' for that deep red pop I can never resist.
      A school of 25 ember tetras and a colony of Amano shrimp round it out. No CO2 injection.
    </p>

    <p>
      My second tank is a 30cm cube — a shrimp-only setup planted with mosses and Bucephalandra,
      home to a colony of Crystal Red Shrimp. Getting them to breed successfully — watching a
      berried female release a cloud of tiny, perfect shrimp into a tank you built specifically for
      them — felt like a genuine achievement. It's become my favourite corner of the hobby.
    </p>

    <h2>Why low-tech?</h2>

    <p>
      I've tried the high-tech route. Pressurised CO2, high-powered lighting, daily dosing
      schedules. It works beautifully — but it's also expensive, time-consuming, and unforgiving.
      For most people who just want a living, thriving, beautiful tank, it's overkill.
    </p>

    <p>
      Low-tech planted tanks are more forgiving, cheaper to run, and easier to maintain — and with
      the right plants and a bit of patience, they can look just as stunning. That's what this site
      is about. I learned the hard way (Glossostigma elatinoides in a low-tech tank: do not do
      this, it will humble you) so you don't have to.
    </p>

    <h2>What you'll find here</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Beginner guides written from actual hands-on experience, not theory.</li>
      <li>Honest troubleshooting — what cloudy water, algae, and plant melt actually mean, and what to do about them.</li>
      <li>Gear recommendations I'd personally use, with real notes on what to skip and why.</li>
      <li>Plant and species guides built around what works in low-tech setups.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      There's a meditative quality to this hobby that I find genuinely grounding. Trimming stems
      on a Sunday morning, watching shrimp forage, seeing a new leaf unfurl on a plant you've
      been nursing. The world outside is loud. The tank just exists, quietly and beautifully, on
      its own schedule.
    </p>

    <p>
      I hope the guides here help you build something you love — and keep your fish alive in
      the process.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
